In this paper, we demonstrate that Graham's two-dimensional grid conjecture implies the Erd\H{o}s--Tur\'{a}n conjecture for arithmetic progressions of length $k = 2s - 1$. Given a set $A \subseteq \{1, \dots, N\}$ of positive upper density, we construct a dense two-dimensional target set $S \subset [N] \times [N]$ via the mapping $(x,y) \in S \iff x + y \in A$. We show that the $s \times s$ grid configurations guaranteed in $S$ by Graham's conjecture force the existence of symmetric system relations in $A$, which collapse directly to a monochromatic arithmetic progression of length $2s - 1$. Consequently, the density bounds prescribed by the two-dimensional grid conjecture yield the sublinear bound $|A| = o(N)$, establishing the Erd\H{o}s--Tur\'{a}n conjecture for all odd progression lengths.
\end{abstract}

